<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/21st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arusha Peace and Reconciliation Agreement for Burundi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2000</span> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundian-civil-war">Burundian Civil War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arusha">Arusha</a> Peace and Reconciliation Agreement for Burundi was signed in Arusha, Tanzania, on 28 August 2000, after talks mediated first by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a> and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>. It set out a transition and a system of power-sharing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> and Tutsi in government, parliament and the army. Most armed groups joined later, and its principles shaped Burundi's constitution of 2005.</p></div>
